{"qid":"Q1194765","names":["Kamarupa Kingdom"],"title":"Kamarupa Kingdom","endonym":null,"capsule":"Kamarupa, also known by its earlier name Pragjyotisha, was the first historically documented kingdom of Assam, controlling the Brahmaputra valley and adjacent hills from the mid-fourth century through the early twelfth. Three successive dynasties held power: the Varman dynasty (c. 350–655), whose king Bhaskaravarman allied with the emperor Harshavardhana of Kanauj against the Gauda kingdom and received the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang at his court around 643; the Mlechchha dynasty (c. 655–900), which shifted the capital toward modern Tezpur; and the Pala dynasty (c. 900–1139), under whom the kingdom maintained extensive contacts with both the Bengal delta and Tibet. At its greatest extent Kamarupa encompassed all of the Brahmaputra valley, much of North Bengal, and parts of what are now Bhutan and Bangladesh. After the death of the last Pala ruler in the early twelfth century, the kingdom dissolved into smaller successor polities — Kamata, Kachari, and Chutiya — whose origins the Ahom kingdom later claimed.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Varman-period core territory in the Brahmaputra valley is reasonably well attested through copper-plate inscriptions, but the northern and southern boundaries of Kamarupa at any given date remain uncertain, and scholarly estimates of its maximum extent vary substantially.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kamarupa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamarupa"},{"label":"Britannica: Kamarupa","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kamarupa"},{"label":"Bri